// The wavefront schedule: the path integrator's steps advanced breadth first,
// through a queue, as MoonRay and pbrt's own wavefront integrator run them.
// scalar.bonsai runs `full_path_step` depth first, its tail call a back edge,
// one sample's whole path before the next sample starts. This runs it breadth
// first: a step that continues its path is an entry on a queue, and once
// every sample of the pixel has taken its first step the queue is drained, a
// round per bounce, every live path of the pixel advancing together. The
// queue is the pixel's -- one per iteration of `p`, sized by the sample
// count -- and its drain is a parfor named `paths`.
//
// The drain is then run sixteen entries at a time: split to the gang width
// with a tail, since a round's batch is however many paths are still alive
// and is rarely a whole number of gangs, and vectorized, so that a gang is
// sixteen paths of one pixel at the same bounce -- packet.bonsai's gang, made
// from the queue rather than from the sample loop, with the same packet
// traversal underneath (vectorized before the traversals below are put on
// their stacks, for the reason packet.bonsai gives). A path that ends leaves
// its lane off for the rest of the round, and the push of the paths that
// continue compacts them into the next round's queue, so its gangs are as
// full as the paths left allow.
//
// What changes against scalar.bonsai is the order a pixel's samples are
// summed in: by bounce rather than by sample, so the film differs by float
// reassociation and no more. compare.sh checks it within a tolerance for
// that reason.
schedule {
    walk_step.loopify();
    path_step.loopify();

    paths = render.queue(p);
    full_path_step.defer(full_path_step, paths);
    render.split(paths, paths_gang, paths_lane, 16, true)
          .vectorize(paths_lane);
    // The producer too: every sample's first step -- its camera ray, its
    // first hit and its first bounce, which end with the push -- is made by
    // a gang of sixteen samples, and its push compacts the paths that
    // continue into the queue's first round. Left scalar, the first bounce
    // was half of a killeroo render's time (PLAN.md, the wavefront's
    // measurements).
    render.split(s, s_gang, s_lane, 16, true)
          .vectorize(s_lane);

    trace.sort(primitives.Interior,
               |i : u8, r : Ray, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / r.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .sort(Inst.blas.Interior,
               |i : u8, r : Ray, axis : u8|
               select((1.0 / r.d)[axis] < 0.0,
                      1.0 - cast[[Float]](i), cast[[Float]](i)))
         .loopify(64);
    trace_any.loopify(64);

    render.bind(p, CPUThread);
}
